This work examines local strong solutions $u$ of the Schr\"odinger equation in the half-space $\mathbb{R}^n_+$ given as:
\begin{align*}
(i\partial_t + \Delta)u = 0, \quad (t,u) \in (0,T]\times \mathbb{R}^n_+,
\end{case
with initial data $f$:
\begin{align*}
u(0,\cdot) = f.
\end{align*}
The analysis assumes $f \in H^s(\mathbb{R}^n_+) \cap C^1(\mathbb{R}^n_+)$ under specific conditions on the regularity indices $s$ and $s+1$. We establish a non-linear short-time blow-up criterion for solutions defined on the spatial domain $\mathbb{R}^n_+$. The proof adapts and demonstrates that the classical demonstration techniques, such as those found in [10], remain effective in this setting despite the existence of the boundary at $x_1=0$. Additionally, we provide a detailed demonstration showing that the classical free-of-boundary Schrodinger blow-up result holds as well. The authors also examine how the boundary condition impacts the classical methods used to demonstrate blow-up. They extend the classical methods to the case with a boundary at $x_1=0$. The authors also
